WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.220
Let's take a cube and dissect it.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.080
We will keep this one as the reference.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.440
Take another cube exactly like this one and dissect it.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.200
By dissect, I mean that we will open the cube up along its edges.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.360
And what will we get if we open it up? Let's see.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.080
We can open the top face first.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.860
This looks like a box with its top face open.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.900
Then we can open the green face in the front.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.480
Then we drop the orange and the blue faces on the right.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.000
The faces that remain are the face to the left and the face behind.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.520
Next, we can drop the left face to the left

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.860
and then we drop the only remaining face behind.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.700
Now we have completely opened up the cube along its edges

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.100
to get a two-dimensional shape like this.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.540
But what is this? What is it called?

00:00:54.680 --> 00:01:00.180
This is called the Net of the solid. In this case, it's the Net of a cube.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.320
A net is a diagram which shows what the solid will look like

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.580
when it is opened along its edges and laid flat.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.320
The net as we can see is clearly two-dimensional.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.880
If we look at it from the top it will look like this.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.360
The orange face is the face to the right.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.080
The blue face is the top face.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.060
The green face is the face in the front. The black face is the base.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.600
The purple one was on the one at the back

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.800
and the red one was on the left.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.840
Yes, all we did was open up the faces along the edges,

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.980
and we got the Net of a solid.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.220
Now listen to the next question carefully.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.780
Does the Net of this solid have to look exactly like this?

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.880
Is this the only possible Net of the Cube?

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.640
Maybe not.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.020
Let's see if we can open up the same cube differently.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.620
We open up the top face first along this edge,

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.320
then we open up the front face Outwards towards the right.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.320
Then we make the side face fall towards the right.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.800
There are still two faces remaining.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.020
The purple one at the back and a red one to the left.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.700
So we drop the left face towards the left

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.700
and the back face backwards.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.700
This is what we get.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.120
It's clearly different from the earlier one.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.020
To know how it's different let's look at the top view.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.780
The top view will look like this.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:27.060
So drawing the net of a solid, may result in different figures.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.360
It doesn't have to be the same.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.780
I am sure you have understood the concept of a net by now.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:38.660
It's easy for me to draw the net of a three-dimensional shape using software.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.380
But how would you draw the net of a solid in your notebook?

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:46.080
Well, you will have to pull out the architect in you.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.920
Look at this cube.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.320
How would you draw the net of this cube in your notebook?

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.620
You don't have 3D software, so you need to imagine.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.300
One good way to draw a net would be by naming the faces.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.760
We can call the face in the front f and the face at the back b.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.920
The face which is down can be called d

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.400
and the one at the top can be called t.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.600
And l and r for left and right

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.520
These are the six faces.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.760
f, b, t, d, l and r.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.820
Now when we draw the Net of this cube,

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:24.080
we should make sure that all faces are covered.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.600
We should start off by having one face as the reference.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.600
Let's move this to the left.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.480
'd' would be a good phase to start off with. We draw base 'd' here.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:38.440
Now we need to imagine as if we are opening up the figure

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:42.600
We can drop 'r' and 't' together towards the right.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:46.060
Yes, we can drop the face on the right and the face on the top.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.920
'r' would be here and 't' would be here next to 'r'.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.660
We have covered three faces and all the three remaining faces

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.420
are attached to the three remaining edges of 't'.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.900
The face in the front when dropped will look like this.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.760
The face on the left when dropped towards the left will look like this.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.580
And the back face will be attached to the top edge of 'd'.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.680
This is one of the ways in which the net of this cube can be drawn.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:17.180
One way to verify it, is by making sure that all the faces

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.800
of the three-dimensional shape are covered.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.340
In the next session we will look at nets of a few more figures.

